<Base
  title="About Swapnil Upganlawar and Model Fieldnotes"
  description="Meet Swapnil Upganlawar, an AI and engineering leader building open-source tools and practical explanations at Model Fieldnotes."
>
  <div class="shell page-content">
    <header class="page-heading">
      <p class="eyebrow">By Swapnil Upganlawar</p>
      <h1>
        Curiosity,
        <br />
        <span>made useful.</span>
      </h1>
      <p>
        I’m Swapnil Upganlawar, an AI and engineering leader focused on agent
        reliability, evaluation, and the economics of useful outcomes. Model
        Fieldnotes is where I build tools and explanations for people taking AI
        beyond the demo.
      </p>
    </header>
    <div class="about-layout">
      <figure class="about-profile">
        <img
          src="/images/swapnil-upganlawar.jpg"
          width="800"
          height="800"
          alt="Swapnil Upganlawar"
        />
        <figcaption>
          <strong>Swapnil Upganlawar</strong>
          <span>Build / Read / Share</span>
          <a
            class="text-link"
            href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/swapnilupganlawar/"
            rel="me"
          >
            Find me on LinkedIn <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span>
          </a>
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
      <div class="prose">
        <h2>A little about me</h2>
        <p>
          I build AI products and lead engineering teams, with experience across
          generative AI, enterprise search, retrieval, personalization, and
          machine learning platforms. I enjoy connecting technical depth with
          product judgment: understanding what a system can do, and deciding
          what is worth building with it.
        </p>
        <p>
          I hold an MBA from Wharton and an MS from Carnegie Mellon. My
          curiosity spans the engineering, economics, and governance of AI: how
          agents behave, how to evaluate models, what useful outcomes cost, and
          where the evidence leaves room for doubt.
        </p>
        <h2>Three questions guide the work</h2>
        <p>
          <strong>Reliability.</strong> Will the agent complete the task
          correctly? The <a href="/fieldbook/">Agent Reliability Fieldbook</a>{' '}
          makes failure mechanisms visible through simulations, with explicit
          limits.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Economics.</strong> What does a useful outcome actually cost?
          I’m interested in the whole task: tools, retries, verification, and
          the work left behind when an attempt fails.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Engineering judgment.</strong> What should we automate, and
          under what controls? Useful evaluations connect technical behavior
          with the product decision a team needs to make.
        </p>
        <h2>A place to work things out</h2>
        <p>
          The work here connects three things: open-source applications you can
          use, research you can inspect, and practical explanations you can
          challenge.
        </p>
        <p>
          <a href="/projects/agent-explainer/">Agent Explainer</a> is the first
          project: an interactive lab for understanding agent failures. The
          fieldnotes explore the surrounding decisions, from retry behavior and
          model evaluation to cost and governance.
        </p>
        <p>
          This is an independent personal project. The writing represents the
          perspective of Model Fieldnotes, and does not speak for an employer,
          vendor, or research institution.
        </p>
        <h2 id="editorial-principles">Editorial principles</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>Follow the evidence.</strong> Link to original papers and
            official documentation. Distinguish source findings from
            interpretation.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Name the limits.</strong> A simulation teaches a mechanism.
            A measured experiment supports a claim within its tested conditions.
            Neither is a universal model ranking.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Show the work.</strong> Reproduced experiments should
            include their setup, model versions, prompts, dates, and
            limitations.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Keep the dates visible.</strong> Pricing, model
            capabilities, and regulation change. Articles show their publication
            and source-review dates.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Correct in the open.</strong> Substantive corrections
            receive an update date and explanation. The source history remains
            available.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Be clear about assistance.</strong> AI assists research,
            drafting, and software development here. Assistance is not
            independent validation; readers can inspect the cited evidence.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <h2>Follow or contribute</h2>
        <p>
          Read signed notes, guides, and research through{' '}
          <a href="/writing/rss.xml">my writing feed</a> or include automated
          briefings with <a href="/rss.xml">the combined feed</a>. Follow{' '}
          <a href="https://github.com/swapupg">swapupg on GitHub</a>, or{' '}
          <a href="https://github.com/swapupg/swapupg.github.io/issues/new">
            suggest a correction or a question
          </a>
          . Contributions that make an explanation clearer are as welcome as
          code.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Base>
